\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{An orthogonal split can lose the determinant sign}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{In the standard orthonormal basis of #{\mathbb C^2}, take
##{K=mI+ib\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\Gamma=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-1\end{pmatrix},\quad m>0,\quad b\in\mathbb R\setminus\{0\}.}
Then #{\operatorname{Re}K=mI}, #{K^*=\Gamma K\Gamma} and #{\det K=m^2+b^2>0}. Compression to the #{+1} eigenspace of the first displayed off-diagonal matrix is the scalar #{m+ib}. This orthogonal splitting does not preserve #{\Gamma}, so the sign conclusion of \ref{fdip-00IZ} does not apply. Its accretive invertibility and the source formula of \ref{fdip-00IX} still apply.}
